Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Specimen Type  Breast core needle biopsy  Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  Ordering Provider  JAMES E PEPPRIELL M D  PH D   CASE  BS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME   Resident  Qinghong Yang  M D   Ph D   Pathologist  Hongbo Yu  M D   Ph D   PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS   PART A 1  RIGHT BREAST  UPPER OUTER  CORE BIOPSIES   ATYPICAL LYMPHOID PROLIFERATION WITH MARKED EOSINOPHILIA  see note   Sections show fibroadipose tissue with nodular lymphoid proliferation composed  mainly of small lymphocytes  There is a polymorphous inflammatory infiltrate  with abundant eosinophils  neutrophils  histiocytes and scattered plasma cells   There are scattered large mononuclear cells  likely immunoblasts  Areas of  necrosis  eosinophil abscesses and giant cell reactions are seen   Immunohistochemical studies performed at BWH show that the lymphocytes are  comprised of a mixture of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   B cells predominantly forming nodules and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    T cells mainly between the nodules  The scattered large mononuclear cells are   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    LCA    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    BSAP    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   BOB 1   OCT 2  and Fascin   likely  immunoblasts  In situ analysis of EBV encoded RNA  EBER  is negative  Desmin  highlights vascular wall but is negative around or within the circular necrotic  areas  Elastic stain highlights vascular wall but is difficult to interpret  whether it is positive around or within the circular necrotic areas  Steiner   Gram  AFB  MSS stains are negative for organisms   NOTE   The findings are of an atypical lymphoid proliferation with marked  eosinophilia  This could represent an intramammary lymph node  Etiology for  marked eosinophilia is not clear  but considerations include response to  infection  parasite exposure or unusual injection of antigenetic protein   Diagnostic features of lymphoproliferative disorder or carcinoma a